# The full triangle group [p, q], the reflection group that generates every regular hyperbolic tiling {p, q}, modeled
# concretely and proved by the kernel. A regular tiling {p, q} is built from one fundamental triangle whose angles are
# pi/p, pi/q, and pi/2. The three reflections in its three sides generate the whole symmetry group, and the tiling is
# the orbit of one triangle under those reflections. This is the hyperbolic sibling of the symmetric group A2 = S3
# proved in group/coxeter/symmetric, where two reflections of the flat triangle gave a finite group. Here three
# reflections of the hyperbolic triangle give an infinite group, the engine behind the pentagrid {5, 4} and the
# heptagrid {7, 3}.
#
# Every triangle group is a Coxeter group. Each generator is an INVOLUTION (a reflection done twice is the identity),
# and each pair of generators satisfies an ORDER relation:
#   (reflect-a reflect-b)^p = identity   (the two sides meeting at the pi/p vertex)
#   (reflect-b reflect-c)^q = identity   (the two sides meeting at the pi/q vertex)
#   (reflect-a reflect-c)^2 = identity   (the two sides meeting at the right-angle vertex)
# The last relation, the order-2 (perpendicular) pair, holds in every regular {p, q} because one angle is always a right
# angle. It is the one relation that closes on a FINITE set of chambers, so the kernel proves it exactly, together with
# the three involutions, below.
#
# A {p, q} is hyperbolic exactly when (p - 2)(q - 2) is above 4. The pentagrid is [5, 4] with (5 - 2)(4 - 2) = 6, and
# the heptagrid is [7, 3] with (7 - 2)(3 - 2) = 5, both above 4 and so both hyperbolic.

# The four chambers around the right-angle vertex of the fundamental triangle, the vertex where sides a and c meet at
# pi/2. A chamber is named by which side of mirror a and which side of mirror c it lies on: `pp` is the positive side of
# both, `pm` positive of a and negative of c, and so on. Reflecting in side a flips the first sign, reflecting in side c
# flips the second, and they act independently because the two mirrors are perpendicular. These four chambers are the
# finite world on which the perpendicular pair closes.
form chamber
  case pp
  case pm
  case mp
  case mm

# TEMPLATE: one entry of a single-valued map on chambers, sending `from` to `to`. Collapses every
# `case .. / send back / make ..` block to a single line per entry, keeping the reflection tables DRY.
tree map-chamber
  take from
  take to

  hook fuse
    case {from}
      send back
        make {to}

# REFLECT-A, the reflection in side a: flip the first sign, leave the second. It swaps the positive and negative sides
# of mirror a and fixes the relation to mirror c.
task reflect-a
  take x, like chamber
  like chamber
  fork case, read x
    fuse map-chamber, pp, mp
    fuse map-chamber, pm, mm
    fuse map-chamber, mp, pp
    fuse map-chamber, mm, pm

# REFLECT-C, the reflection in side c: flip the second sign, leave the first. Perpendicular to reflect-a, so it acts on
# the other coordinate.
task reflect-c
  take x, like chamber
  like chamber
  fork case, read x
    fuse map-chamber, pp, pm
    fuse map-chamber, pm, pp
    fuse map-chamber, mp, mm
    fuse map-chamber, mm, mp

# REFLECT-B, the reflection in side b, the third side of the triangle: the diagonal mirror that exchanges the two
# coordinates. It swaps the role of side a and side c, sending pm to mp and back, and fixes the chambers already on the
# diagonal. It is a genuine involution, the third Coxeter generator.
task reflect-b
  take x, like chamber
  like chamber
  fork case, read x
    fuse map-chamber, pp, pp
    fuse map-chamber, pm, mp
    fuse map-chamber, mp, pm
    fuse map-chamber, mm, mm

# REFLECT-A IS AN INVOLUTION: reflect-a (reflect-a x) = x. A reflection done twice returns every chamber to itself, the
# defining property of a Coxeter generator. Proved over all four chambers.
rule reflect-a-is-an-involution
  seat x, like chamber
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-a
        call reflect-a
          read x
      read x
  fold x

# REFLECT-B IS AN INVOLUTION: reflect-b (reflect-b x) = x.
rule reflect-b-is-an-involution
  seat x, like chamber
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-b
        call reflect-b
          read x
      read x
  fold x

# REFLECT-C IS AN INVOLUTION: reflect-c (reflect-c x) = x.
rule reflect-c-is-an-involution
  seat x, like chamber
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-c
        call reflect-c
          read x
      read x
  fold x

# THE ORDER-2 RELATION: (reflect-a reflect-c)^2 = identity. Sides a and c are perpendicular, so their composite is an
# order-2 rotation, and doing it twice is the identity:
# reflect-a (reflect-c (reflect-a (reflect-c x))) = x. This is the third Coxeter relation of [p, q], the one that holds
# in every regular tiling because one triangle angle is always a right angle. Proved over all four chambers.
rule reflect-a-then-c-has-order-two
  seat x, like chamber
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-a
        call reflect-c
          call reflect-a
            call reflect-c
              read x
      read x
  fold x

# THE PERPENDICULAR PAIR COMMUTES: reflect-a (reflect-c x) = reflect-c (reflect-a x). Equivalent to the order-2 relation
# given the involutions, and the cleanest witness that mirrors a and c are independent. Proved over all four chambers.
rule perpendicular-reflections-commute
  seat x, like chamber
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-a
        call reflect-c
          read x
      call reflect-c
        call reflect-a
          read x
  fold x

# A CLOSED WORD, a relator of the presentation: reflect-a reflect-c reflect-a reflect-c is the identity word on the
# chambers, returning pp to pp. A concrete witness that this length-four word in the generators is trivial in the group.
rule order-two-word-fixes-a-chamber
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-a
        call reflect-c
          call reflect-a
            call reflect-c
              make pp
      make pp
  calm hold

# THE PENTAGRID IS [5, 4] AND HYPERBOLIC. Its fundamental triangle has angles pi/5, pi/4, pi/2, and the Schlafli test
# (p - 2)(q - 2) above 4 gives (5 - 2)(4 - 2) = 3 * 2 = 6, above 4. The order-p relation is (reflect-a reflect-b)^5 and
# the order-q relation is (reflect-b reflect-c)^4.
hold pentagrid-triangle-group-is-hyperbolic
  call is-above
    call multiply
      call subtract
        code 5
        code 2
      call subtract
        code 4
        code 2
    code 4

# THE HEPTAGRID IS [7, 3] AND HYPERBOLIC. Its fundamental triangle has angles pi/7, pi/3, pi/2, and the Schlafli test
# gives (7 - 2)(3 - 2) = 5 * 1 = 5, above 4. The order-p relation is (reflect-a reflect-b)^7 and the order-q relation is
# (reflect-b reflect-c)^3.
hold heptagrid-triangle-group-is-hyperbolic
  call is-above
    call multiply
      call subtract
        code 7
        code 2
      call subtract
        code 3
        code 2
    code 4

# THE FLAT CASE [4, 4] IS THE BORDERLINE, NOT HYPERBOLIC. The square tiling has (4 - 2)(4 - 2) = 4, which is not above 4,
# the exact threshold the hyperbolic tilings must exceed. This pins down why the pentagrid and heptagrid open up while
# the square grid stays flat.
hold square-tiling-is-not-hyperbolic
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      call subtract
        code 4
        code 2
      call subtract
        code 4
        code 2
    code 4
